+(a forge API call, an MCP call), the *harness* is what presents credentials
+on its behalf. Only the pod tier holds keys; everything below is signed
+claims. No per-subagent key material, no PoP problem below the pod.
+
+*What* the harness presents at that hop is an open question, not a settled
+one, and the next subsection works it out. It matters more than it looks:
+how it resolves decides how much of the issuer apparatus above is
+load-bearing.
+
+### The outbound hop: what the harness actually presents
+
+There are **two** boundaries here, not one, and conflating them is the main
+way this design goes wrong. The MCP servers in scope are ToolHive vMCPs, and
+vMCP is a *gateway*. So hop 1 is mecatl to vMCP, where both ends are ours and
+we choose the protocol. Hop 2 is vMCP to each backend MCP server, where each
+backend has its own idea of a valid credential and **most will not trust vMCP
+as an issuer**. That is the normal case, not the exception. What we choose at
+hop 1 constrains what is available at hop 2.
+
+At hop 1 there is one credential and two options.
+
+**Option 1: present at vMCP's own token endpoint.** mecatl authenticates as a
+registered OAuth client and exchanges the user's token for a vMCP-minted one.
+One client-authentication path covers both grants the harness needs:
+`client_credentials` when there is no user (which is what a scheduled fire
+needs, since it has no user by construction), and `token-exchange` when there
+is. The delegated branch is ordinary RFC 8693 into the hardened handler
+ToolHive already ships, which checks consent fail-closed, intersects scopes,
+narrows the audience and caps `act` nesting depth. Nothing here needs the
+front-door IdP to know about SPIFFE, because the AS checking the credential
+is ours.
+
+The precondition is a feature: the consent check requires the subject token
+to carry a `may_act.sub` naming mecatl or a matching `client_id`, and rejects
+an unbound token outright. So it works when the token was minted *for* mecatl
+and not when mecatl merely holds someone's token. RFC 9068 §2.2 makes
+`client_id` REQUIRED in conformant JWT access tokens, so that arm needs no
+vendor feature and no `may_act`, which is just as well since `may_act` is
+close to dead in deployment.
+
+**Option 2: ID-JAG at the front-door IdP,** for callers whose token does not
+name mecatl at all. The harness holds the user's ID token, does ID-JAG Step A
+to get an assertion scoped to vMCP's AS, and redeems it there. The IdP decides
+whether mecatl may act, out of cross-app consent.
+
+**What separates them is who else has to cooperate,** not how much code it
+takes. Option 1 needs nothing from any party outside ToolHive. Option 2 needs
+the front-door IdP to implement ID-JAG Step A, which Okta does and others do
+not, so its availability is not ours to decide. Option 1 also rests on a
+ratified RFC where Option 2 rests on a draft. So: **Option 1 as the default,
+Option 2 as an addition.** Both land at the same endpoint and the issued token
+is identical either way, so the gateway policy rules and the subagent story
+get written once.
+
+**Three blockers, all on the ToolHive side, and none of them the one you would
+expect.** Verified against `main`:
+
+1. **No client that may use the exchange grant can be provisioned.** The
+   handler rejects public clients and requires the grant registered. Dynamic
+   registration is the only provisioning path, and it hardcodes clients as
+   public, rejects any `token_endpoint_auth_method` other than `none`, and
+   permits only `authorization_code` and `refresh_token`. There is no
+   static-client config. These gates are identical on every branch, so the
+   shipped delegation path is reachable today only through a test seam.
+   Fixing it is small: relax registration, or add a static client.
+2. **The subject token must be issued by ToolHive's own AS.** Only a
+   self-issued validator is wired; the multi-issuer validator in the tree has
+   no callers and an open TODO. So an Okta or Keycloak access token cannot
+   currently be the `subject_token`, which is what Option 1 assumes it is.
+   This is the harder of the two and gates the whole flow.
+3. **Client authentication is not the blocker.** ToolHive's AS advertises only
+   `none` and its OAuth library does not implement `tls_client_auth`, so the
+   mTLS shape needs new code. But `client_secret_basic` already works with no
+   new code, because a confidential client is not an OpenID Connect client and
+   the method switch is skipped. A first cut needs neither SPIFFE nor mTLS.
+
+### What the SVID buys at this hop, stated narrowly
+
+Under Option 2 the SVID does nothing at hop 1: Step B skips client
+authentication, and using it at Step A would need the IdP to speak
+`jwt-spiffe`, which Okta and Entra do not implement. And it is not the case
+that the alternative is a static secret, since ID-JAG permits
+`private_key_jwt` at Step A. What would sit idle is the SPIFFE machinery
+specifically: trust domain, bundle distribution, Workload-API rotation.
+
+Under Option 1 it buys two things, both real and both smaller than "the
+harness's identity":
+
+- **No static `client_secret` in mecatl's environment.** This is concrete
+  rather than theoretical: `envscrub` exists precisely because under posture
+  `auto` or `yolo` the model can read the harness's own environment.
+- **Per-pod attestation,** so a compromised image inherits no shared secret.
+
+And one thing it buys that nothing else does, which is the strongest argument
+for it: **`client_id` becomes the SPIFFE URI.** The shipped mint puts the
+OAuth `client_id` in `act.sub` and does no SPIFFE parsing, so whether
+`act.sub` holds a SPIFFE ID depends entirely on whether `client_id` is one.
+With `client_secret_basic` it is not. This is the only mechanism that makes a
+SPIFFE-shaped authorization policy real, so the SPIFFE client-auth path is an
+enhancement over a working exchange rather than a precondition for one.
+
+Worth recording that no SPIFFE-awareness exists in the gateway's policy layer
+today. Cedar is claim-generic, and the SPIFFE IDs in its test fixtures are
+matched by a string glob that would treat any other prefix identically.
 
